    "https://employment.gonzaga.edu/jobs/visiting-research-scholar-school-of-education-spokane-washington-united-states": {
      "title": "Visiting Research Scholar, School of Education",
      "description": "Visiting Research Scholar, School of Education - Spokane, Washington, United States Visiting Research Scholar, School of Education - Spokane, Washington, United States Kiosk mode We're Hiring! Visiting Research Scholar, School of Education Spokane, Washington, United States Visiting Faculty Closing at: Nov 13 2026 - 23:55 PST Title: Visiting Research Scholar, School of Education Area: School of Education Department: Teacher Education Telework Availability: On-campus Position Employee Classification: Visiting Faculty Appointment Percentage: 100 Months per year: NA - FXD/Temp Position Salary Range: The budgeted salary that the University reasonably expects to offer for this position is $43,330 (w/out PhD) or $54,990 (w/ PhD). The internal salary ranges are $43,330 - $44,630 (w/out PhD) and $54,990 - $56,639 (w/ PhD). Budgeted salary is based on the full appointment period (January 2, 2027 - August 15, 2027) and may be prorated depending on the start date. Close Date: November 13, 2026 Is this position eligible for relocation reimbursement?: No Is this position eligible for a signing bonus?: No Gonzaga University evaluates request for H1-B sponsorship on a case by case basis. Sponsorship eligibility depends on the position, institutional considerations, and applicable federal immigration requirements. Job Purpose: The School of Education at Gonzaga University in Spokane, WA invites applications for a short-term, non-tenure-track Visiting Research Scholar with an appointment beginning January 2, 2027 through August 15, 2027. The Visiting Scholar will play two connected roles. First, the Scholar will assist with research and programming for the School of Education’s Gates Foundation–funded work, including the “AI for Instruction Fellowship,” which provides outreach and professional development to K-12 educators around intentional, ethical use of AI. Responsibilities will include identifying relevant research, supporting data collection and analysis, and helping design and facilitate professional learning for preservice and practicing teachers and school leaders. Second, the Scholar will lead or contribute to a focal project matched to their interests and expertise in one of three areas: Multilingual learner education – examining how AI can support asset-based instruction for multilingual learners, including language and content learning, translanguaging, and family–school communication, with attention to linguistic bias, equitable access, and the preparation of teachers to serve culturally and linguistically diverse students. Adolescent development and AI – contributing to work on adolescent AI literacy, drawing on research in adolescent development, youth participatory action research, and critical perspectives on AI. Special education – contributing to an in-progress design project that reimagines the Individualized Education Program (IEP) for the age of artificial intelligence. The project aims to design a next-generation IEP framework that uses AI selectively to reduce administrative burden while shifting the focus from deficit-based compliance toward student agency and self-determination, meaningful student and family participation, and attention to the intersecting inequities of race and disability. Across all focal areas, AI is treated as a support for professional judgment and human collaboration rather than an automated substitute, with explicit attention to accuracy, bias, privacy, and legal compliance. Applicants may include advanced doctoral candidates nearing completion, recent Ph.D. graduates, or established scholars whose work aligns with the mission and priorities of the School of Education. Grounded in Gonzaga’s Jesuit, Catholic, and humanistic mission, the School of Education prepares educators to serve every learner with dignity and care. As artificial intelligence reshapes classrooms, we believe the most consequential questions it raises in education are not only technical but human—questions of equity, agency, and the relationships among students, families, and teachers. This work asks how AI can support teaching and lighten administrative burden without displacing professional judgment, how design choices can advance rather than diminish the agency of multilingual learners, adolescents, and students with disabilities, and how the next generation of educators can be equipped not merely to use AI but to shape it wisely and justly. The Visiting Scholar will help translate that vision into actionable steps for K-12 teachers. Gonzaga University is a Catholic, Jesuit, and humanistic institution grounded in a mission to form the whole person — intellectually, ethically, and spiritually — and to prepare graduates for lives of leadership and service for the common good. Rooted in the Ignatian tradition of cura personalis (care for the whole person) and \"faith that does justice,\" Gonzaga calls its faculty, staff, and students into a vibrant community committed to social justice, solidarity, and finding meaning and purpose in their work. Located in Spokane, Washington — the heart of the Inland Northwest — the university offers an exceptional quality of life in one of the Pacific Northwest's most livable and dynamic mid-sized cities. Spokane sits at the crossroads of mountains and rivers, offering four distinct seasons, world-class outdoor recreation from skiing and hiking to whitewater and cycling, and a thriving downtown with tremendous sports events as well as arts and culinary scene. With a cost of living well below that of larger coastal metros and a strong sense of community, Spokane combines the amenities of a city with the warmth and accessibility of a place where people genuinely know their neighbors — making it an ideal home for those seeking both purposeful work and an exceptional place to visit and live. For more information on our mission, please visit: https://www.gonzaga.edu/about/our-mission-jesuit-values/mission-statement Essential Functions: Gates Grant Research and Programming – Assist with research and programming for the Gates Foundation “AI for Instruction Fellowship” and related K-12 AI literacy work. Identify and synthesize relevant research. Support data collection, analysis, and reporting. Help design, facilitate, and evaluate professional learning for preservice and practicing teachers and school leaders (e.g., AI Academy sessions, professional development workshops, school-leader convenings, and a spring showcase). Focal Project Research and Design – Collaborate with faculty to lead or contribute to a project in the Scholar’s area of expertise (multilingual learner education, adolescent development and AI, or special education). Build a knowledge base of relevant research and an asset-oriented vision to guide AI applications in that area. Develop a deliverable product (e.g., paper, framework, prototype, curriculum, website, or app) to disseminate the recommendations. (There is also the possibility of designing an original research project.) Collaboration and Dissemination – Contribute to the intellectual life of the School of Education by collaborating with faculty. Share developments through seminars, workshops, presentations, and written products for educators, families, and scholarly audiences. Minimum Qualifications: Advanced doctoral candidate, recent Ph.D. graduate, or established scholar with a background in AI and/or educational technology. Expertise in one or more of the following focal areas: Multilingual learner education (e.g., second language acquisition, bilingual and dual language education, culturally and linguistically sustaining pedagogy); Adolescent development and AI (e.g., youth digital and AI literacy, middle-level education, youth participatory research) Special education (e.g., disability studies in education, DisCrit, self-determination, family–school collaboration). Preferred Qualifications: Ability to collaborate with faculty, staff, students, and partners across disciplines and sectors. Experience with educational research methods (e.g., qualitative, survey, or mixed methods) and with supporting collaborative research or programming. Experience translating research into educational resources, workshops, or prototypes. Educational Requirement: Master's Degree Physical & On-Campus Requirements: In-Office Presence - Ability to work on-site in typical office setting and participate in in-person meetings with colleagues: 5 days/week Standing/Sitting - Ability to remain in a stationary position for a prolonged period: Daily (>75% of time) Walking - Ability to move about on foot to accomplish tasks: Daily (>75% of time) Lifting/Carrying - Ability to move or transport objects up to a specified weight: Rarely (1%-49% of time) Attention to Detail - Ability to focus on detailed tasks for extended periods: Daily (>75% of time) Problem-Solving - Ability to identify and resolve issues effectively: Daily (>75% of time) Required Application Materials: To apply, please visit our website at: https://www.gonzaga.edu/jobs. Applicants must complete an online application and submit the following: Curriculum vitae. Research statement (2–3 pages) describing: a. current and recent research activities; b. experience in one or more of the focal areas (multilingual learner education, adolescent development and AI, or special education) and with the use of AI in education; c. future research interests and how they align with the mission and priorities of Gonzaga University’s School of Education. Statement of educational innovation and interdisciplinary engagement (1–2 pages) describing: a. experience translating research into educational, training, or experiential learning opportunities; b. experience collaborating across disciplines, sectors, or stakeholder groups; c. how the candidate's work could contribute to Gonzaga University's Jesuit, Catholic, and humanistic mission. Cover letter addressing the candidate's interest in the position, qualifications for the role, and the focal area(s) they would pursue. Names and contact information for three (3) professional references will be collected from advancing candidates during the interview process (letters of recommendation will be requested when finalists are selected). For information about the institution, visit Gonzaga on the internet at www.gonzaga.edu . Questions about the position can be directed to Anny Case, School of Education, casea@gonzaga.edu. This position will close November 13, 2026 at 11:55PM (PST).For assistance with your online application, please call the Human Resources Department at 509-313-5996. Total Rewards Statement At Gonzaga, we value the well-being and contributions of our colleagues by offering a comprehensive total rewards package. In compliance with Washington State's Pay Transparency Act, we provide both the full salary range for each position, and an anticipated salary range that reflects the budgeted amount that the university reasonable expects to offer the position. In addition to base compensation, we offer a competitive benefits package, which may vary depending on the position type and hours worked. To explore the full range of benefits available, please review the applicable Prospective Employee Benefit Guide available on the Gonzaga website. EEO Statement Gonzaga University is a Jesuit, Catholic, humanistic institution, and is therefore interested in candidates who will contribute to its distinctive mission. Gonzaga University is a committed EEO/AA employer and diversity candidates are encouraged to apply. All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to their disability status and/or protected veteran status. Applicants with disabilities needing reasonable accommodations to complete the application or hiring process should contact Human Resources (509) 313-5996. Apply now First Name (required) 42dbdc0b Last Name (required) e94964e9 Email (required) ef4a2773 Apply Please wait... Done Not You? Thank you Recommended jobs Visiting Research Scholar, School of Education Spokane, Washington, United States School of Education The School of Education at Gonzaga University in Spokane, WA invites applications for a short-term, non-tenure-track Visiting Research Scholar for a 7.5-month appointment beginning January 2, 2027 and continuing through August 15, 2027. The Visiting Scholar will play two connected roles. First, the Scholar will assist with research and programming for the School of Education’s Gates Foundation–funded work, including the “AI for Instruction Fellowship,” which provides outreach and professional development to K-12 educators around intentional, ethical use of AI. Security Officer Spokane, Washington, United States Campus Security & Public Safety Gonzaga University's Campus Security team is hiring for a Security Officer! Head Coach - Women's Cross Country, Track & Field Spokane, Washington, United States Other Women's Sports Lead and shape a nationally competitive NCAA Division I Women's Cross Country and Track & Field program while competing in the highly competitive Pac-12 Conference. This position is responsible for all aspects of program administration, including coaching, student-athlete development, recruiting, mentoring, program planning, public relations, community engagement, and administrative oversight. The successful candidate will foster a culture of excellence on and off the track while ensuring full compliance with all University, conference, and NCAA regulations. Adjunct Instructor, Accounting (Taxation) Spokane, Washington, United States School of Business Administration Gonzaga University is seeking adjunct faculty members in the School of Business Administration to fulfill open course opportunities in the Spring 2027 semester for our Master of Science in Taxation program. Adjunct Instructor, School of Business Administration Spokane, Washington, United States School of Business Administration Gonzaga University in Spokane, Washington, is seeking adjunct faculty members in the School of Business Administration to fulfill open course opportunities in the Spring 2027 semester. Adjunct Instructor, Teacher Education Spokane, Washington, United States School of Education The Teacher Education program at Gonzaga University in Spokane, Washington, invites applications for its Adjunct Instructor Pool focused on Washington State teacher certification coursework. We welcome applications from individuals with 5–7 years of K–12 teaching experience who demonstrate specialized expertise in K–12 educational practice. Return to Job Search Sign up for job alerts Job Alert b6029f2b Finalize your job alert by selecting criteria from the dropdowns below. You can select multiple options from each dropdown by returning to the combobox and re-entering the list of options. Submit at the end to create your job alert. Departments School of Education School of Education Locations Spokane, Washington, United States Spokane, Washington, United States First Name (required) 83459870 Last Name (required) 9c7c9092 Email (required) ea386e5d Send Please wait... Done Not You? Thank you Candidate Details We use cookies. Some are necessary to operate the website and its functions. Others help personalize, improve content and services to show you the most relevant job opportunities. With the decision \"Accept essential only\" we will respect your privacy and will not set cookies that aren't necessary for the operation of the site. Accept essential only Accept all Please choose the location(s)"
